to be infected with it.

197
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:23,180
In those areas the infection
can also produce a flu

198
00:11:23,180 --> 00:11:25,560
-like illness and pneumonia
that's referred to as

199
00:11:25,560 --> 00:11:29,340
valley fever and then tends
to subside on its own.

200
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:33,740
But for those with suppressed
immunity, disseminated

201
00:11:33,740 --> 00:11:36,460
disease can occur
also, and that has the

202
00:11:36,460 --> 00:11:39,140
potential to affect almost
any organ in the body.

203
00:11:42,440 --> 00:11:45,740
So here are the steps that
occur during infection.

204
00:11:46,070 --> 00:11:48,980
Now once it's in the
lungs, the spores transform

205
00:11:48,980 --> 00:11:52,100
into these thick-walled
structures called spherules

206
00:11:52,100 --> 00:11:55,220
that are filled with
endospores, and you can see two

207
00:11:55,220 --> 00:11:57,900
representative images of
those on the side here.

208
00:11:58,230 --> 00:12:01,060
Now, when those rupture,
the endospores are going to

209
00:12:01,060 --> 00:12:04,580
be released and differentiate
to form new spherules.

210
00:12:04,860 --> 00:12:08,980
Now, the wall of a spherule
also contains a glycoprotein

211
00:12:08,980 --> 00:12:11,720
layer that acts as a
physical shield to prevent

212
00:12:11,720 --> 00:12:15,580
phacocytosis, but in most
healthy individuals, the

213
00:12:15,580 --> 00:12:18,280
immune system contains the
infection locally in the

214
00:12:18,280 --> 00:12:22,040
lungs with a cell-mediated
response, and so you get

215
00:12:22,040 --> 00:12:26,100
either an asymptomatic
case or one having mild flu

216
00:12:26,100 --> 00:12:30,380
-like symptoms, with some
developing erythema nodosum,

217
00:12:30,440 --> 00:12:34,800
tender skin nodules that
result from the immune-mediated

218
00:12:34,800 --> 00:12:37,760
inflammatory response to
that initial pulmonary

219
00:12:37,760 --> 00:12:42,960
infection, which is not a
sign of disseminated disease.

220
00:12:43,330 --> 00:12:46,220
But in a small number of
cases, the organism can

221
00:12:46,220 --> 00:12:49,880
disseminate via the bloodstream,
which can basically go

222
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:55,540
anywhere. but most often it
affects the skin, bones, joints,

223
00:12:55,580 --> 00:12:59,440
and meninges. So the clinical
picture here is somewhat

224
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:03,340
broad, ranging from
meningitis to skin granulomas.

225
00:13:06,100 --> 00:13:10,300
So here is a representation
of skin lesions that

226
00:13:10,300 --> 00:13:13,480
occurred due to the disseminated
spread of the organism.

227
00:13:13,900 --> 00:13:17,360
The incidence of dissemination
is higher in certain

228
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:20,380
groups of people, like
Filipinos or African

229
00:13:20,380 --> 00:13:23,940
-Americans, and the reason
for that is not really

230
00:13:23,940 --> 00:13:27,200
all that well understood.
It likely has something

231
00:13:27,200 --> 00:13:29,860
to do with their particular
genetic background.

232
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:33,220
Women late in
pregnancy also have an

233
00:13:33,220 --> 00:13:36,240
increased risk of
dissemination, as well as

234
00:13:36,240 --> 00:13:38,070
those that are somehow
immunocompromised.

235
00:13:40,100 --> 00:13:42,760
Diagnosis can be made
by identifying the

236
00:13:42,760 --> 00:13:46,000
organism from sputum
or a tissue biopsy,

237
00:13:46,340 --> 00:13:49,140
And because it's also
highly infectious,

238
00:13:49,140 --> 00:13:51,580
typically culturing it
is not going to be done.

239
00:13:51,700 --> 00:13:55,420
But serological testing
can be performed like an

240
00:13:55,420 --> 00:14:01,640
ELISA to detect IgM or IgG
antibodies or an assessment

241
00:14:01,640 --> 00:14:05,120
of the IgG titer with a
complement fixation test.

242
00:14:05,550 --> 00:14:06,760
And of course, there's always

243
00:14:06,760 --> 00:14:08,600
PCR that can be done as well.

244
00:14:08,960 --> 00:14:13,760
Now, since most cases are
asymptomatic, no treatment

245
00:14:13,760 --> 00:14:18,140
is necessary there. but
fluconazole or itraconazole can

246
00:14:18,140 --> 00:14:21,820
be used for persistent lung
infections or mild cases.

247
00:14:22,360 --> 00:14:25,000
And in a situation
where there's severe

248
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,160
dissemination,
amphotericin B is used.

249
00:14:28,320 --> 00:14:31,040
And in cases of
meningitis, fluconazole

250
00:14:31,040 --> 00:14:33,940
is used, but typically
the results are

251
00:14:33,940 --> 00:14:35,940
going to be relatively
poor for that.

252
00:14:38,980 --> 00:14:43,100
our second example here
is histoplasma capsulatum

253
00:14:43,100 --> 00:14:46,220
which is the most common
pulmonary fungal infection in

254
00:14:46,220 --> 00:14:50,300
the u.s it's endemic in
the midwestern and central

255
00:14:50,300 --> 00:14:53,980
united states specifically
within the ohio and

256
00:14:53,980 --> 00:14:58,280
mississippi river valleys but
it could also be seen as far

257
00:14:58,280 --> 00:15:01,240
south as texas like you
could see here in the map.

258
00:15:01,560 --> 00:15:05,380
Now, in these very highly
endemic areas, a large portion

259
00:15:05,380 --> 00:15:09,120
of the population can be
infected with the organism.

260
00:15:09,120 --> 00:15:13,340
Upwards of about 85% of
individuals can test positively

261
00:15:13,340 --> 00:15:17,060
sometime during their
lifetime, but most of those

262
00:15:17,060 --> 00:15:19,780
people won't develop any
sort of noticeable symptoms.

263
00:15:20,620 --> 00:15:23,380
Now, there are also
two cerevars of the

264
00:15:23,380 --> 00:15:27,540
species. The Duboisii
form is endemic to central

265
00:15:27,540 --> 00:15:30,940
and western Africa
and unlike U.S.

266
00:15:30,980 --> 00:15:34,320
histoplasmosis which
primarily infects the lungs

267
00:15:34,320 --> 00:15:37,400
this other variant targets
other locations in the

268
00:15:37,400 --> 00:15:42,180
body like the skin and
the bones now histoplasma

269
00:15:42,180 --> 00:15:45,400
is thermally dimorphic
like you can see here in

270
00:15:45,400 --> 00:15:48,580
the microscopic microscopic
images on the slide

271
00:15:49,250 --> 00:15:52,380
and when it's growing
as a mold with

272
00:15:52,380 --> 00:15:55,820
hyphae it will produce
two types of spores

273
00:15:56,580 --> 00:15:59,880
Macroconidia, that have
thick walls and little

274
00:15:59,880 --> 00:16:03,360
finger-like projections,
but don't lead to infection.

275
00:16:03,500 --> 00:16:07,360
And then microconidia,
which are smaller, thin and

276
00:16:07,360 --> 00:16:10,820
smooth-walled spores that,
when inhaled, will actually

277
00:16:10,820 --> 00:16:14,940
transmit the disease or
provide the infection.

278
00:16:15,590 --> 00:16:18,400
Both of those conidia
can be seen in the

279
00:16:18,400 --> 00:16:21,660
image here as the larger
and smaller circles

280
00:16:21,660 --> 00:16:24,200
when the fungus is
grown at 25 degrees.

281
00:16:25,180 --> 00:16:26,880
Now in terms of
the environment,

282
00:16:27,200 --> 00:16:30,240
it proliferates best in
soil contaminated with

283
00:16:30,240 --> 00:16:33,780
bird or bat droppings,
which will alter the soil

284
00:16:33,780 --> 00:16:36,160
composition and lead to
an increase in nitrogen

285
00:16:36,160 --> 00:16:39,020
content that favors the
growth of the organism.

286
00:16:39,320 --> 00:16:42,380
So sites that are commonly
associated with exposure

287
00:16:42,380 --> 00:16:45,740
to histoplasma are going to
be locations like chicken

288
00:16:45,740 --> 00:16:49,660
coops or abandoned buildings
with roosting sites

289
00:16:49,660 --> 00:16:53,580
as well as caves where you
would find bats and this

290
00:16:53,580 --> 00:16:57,580
is how it gets its alternate
name of cave disease and

291
00:16:57,580 --> 00:17:00,580
sometimes it's also referred
to as ohio river valley

292
00:17:00,580 --> 00:17:04,900
disease and so associated
activities in these areas

293
00:17:04,900 --> 00:17:08,960
would increase the risk of
exposure like excavation

294
00:17:08,960 --> 00:17:12,740
and demolition for a
construction project or even

295
00:17:12,740 --> 00:17:15,920
something as simple as
just exploring a cave heavy

296
00:17:16,100 --> 00:17:20,300
exposure usually occurs
in an enclosed area like a

297
00:17:20,300 --> 00:17:25,820
cave or like the attic of
an old house, for instance.

298
00:17:25,900 --> 00:17:28,840
And from there, the
severity of the symptoms

299
00:17:28,840 --> 00:17:31,540
and the course of the
disease are going to depend

300
00:17:31,540 --> 00:17:34,060
on the extent of the
exposure and then also

301
00:17:34,060 --> 00:17:36,740
the immune status of
the infected individual.

302
00:17:39,980 --> 00:17:43,640
Histoplasma infection is
usually self-limiting and

303
00:17:43,640 --> 00:17:46,360
asymptomatic, but for
those with weakened immune

304
00:17:46,360 --> 00:17:49,560
systems, severe pneumonia
or disseminated disease can

305
00:17:49,560 --> 00:17:53,420
occur. Now when the spores
are inhaled, they'll grow

306
00:17:53,420 --> 00:17:57,340
as yeast and then they'll
be engulfed by macrophages,

307
00:17:57,340 --> 00:18:00,620
but aren't killed
efficiently. The yeast cells

308
00:18:00,620 --> 00:18:04,420
survive within phageolysosomes
by raising the pH and

309
00:18:04,420 --> 00:18:08,120
inactivating enzymes within
the compartment. And you

310
00:18:08,120 --> 00:18:11,760
can see here on the
microscopic image, which shows

311
00:18:11,760 --> 00:18:15,220
the yeast cells persisting
inside the macrophages.

312
00:18:15,960 --> 00:18:18,980
Once cellular immunity begins

313
00:18:18,980 --> 00:18:20,740
and the response occurs,

314
00:18:20,880 --> 00:18:23,960
it can result in the
development of granulomas at

315
00:18:23,960 --> 00:18:28,600
various sites like lymph
nodes, liver or spleen,

316
00:18:28,600 --> 00:18:32,260
or the lungs like you can
see here on the CT scan.

317
00:18:32,920 --> 00:18:36,300
Cells can lie dormant in
these granulomas, even for

318
00:18:36,300 --> 00:18:39,440
years, if they're not healed
through calcification.

319
00:18:39,840 --> 00:18:44,400
And then later, if the
immune system weakens, the

320
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:46,560
cells can escape the
granulomas and spread

321
00:18:46,560 --> 00:18:49,200
throughout the body, causing
disseminated infection.

322
00:18:49,320 --> 00:18:51,880
And actually, the
majority of histoplasmosis

323
00:18:51,880 --> 00:18:56,060
cases in non-endemic areas
occurs as a result of

324
00:18:56,060 --> 00:18:58,820
the reactivation of latent
infection like this.

325
00:18:59,500 --> 00:19:03,460
Cell-mediated immunity plays
a main role in containing

326
00:19:03,460 --> 00:19:06,460
the infection. The
response leads to the

327
00:19:06,460 --> 00:19:12,260
production of cytokines like
IL-12, TNF-alpha, interferon

328
00:19:12,260 --> 00:19:15,840
gamma, and those are going
to activate macrophages

329
00:19:15,840 --> 00:19:18,780
and enhance their ability
to kill histoplasma.

330
00:19:18,780 --> 00:19:21,760
And this amplifies the
immune response against

331
00:19:21,760 --> 00:19:25,540
the organism and halts any
sort of disease progression

332
00:19:25,540 --> 00:19:28,860
that you would find in
a healthy individual.

333
00:19:29,330 --> 00:19:32,940
Now, failure to activate
that response is a key factor

334
00:19:32,940 --> 00:19:36,000
in patients who develop
disseminated histoplasmosis.

335
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:39,500
So patients with underlying
conditions like AIDS

336
00:19:39,500 --> 00:19:43,320
are at risk for developing
more severe and potentially

337
00:19:43,320 --> 00:19:47,580
fatal progressive forms of
the infection. The exact

338
00:19:47,580 --> 00:19:50,700
clinical presentation of
disseminated disease can

339
00:19:50,700 --> 00:19:55,680
vary, potentially involving
the liver and spleen, the

340
00:19:55,680 --> 00:19:59,300
skin, the GI tract, and
the central nervous system.

341
00:20:02,500 --> 00:20:06,300
There are a few ways to
diagnose histoplasma, including

342
00:20:06,300 --> 00:20:09,960
tissue biopsies and other
samples to microscopically

343
00:20:09,960 --> 00:20:14,060
view yeast within macrophages
or growing it in culture.

344
00:20:14,060 --> 00:20:19,180
An enzyme immunoassay to
detect antigen in urine

345
00:20:19,180 --> 00:20:23,060
or blood is possible, along
with antibody tests like

346
00:20:23,060 --> 00:20:28,180
complement fixation or
immunodiffusion, which tests for

347
00:20:28,180 --> 00:20:31,640
precipitating antibodies
that form two bands in an

348
00:20:31,640 --> 00:20:36,440
agar gel, one for IgGs against
the mycelial phase antigen

349
00:20:36,440 --> 00:20:40,220
and another for IgGs against
a yeast phase antigen.

350
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:44,260
Now, in terms of treatment,
if it's needed for

351
00:20:44,260 --> 00:20:47,240
mild or moderate disease,
itraconazole could

352
00:20:47,240 --> 00:20:51,200
be used as an initial
therapy, and amphotericin

353
00:20:51,200 --> 00:20:53,400
B would be the initial
treatment for patients

354
00:20:53,400 --> 00:20:57,060
who have more severe or
disseminated disease.

355
00:21:00,720 --> 00:21:04,700
Blastomyces dermatitis
really mirrors much of what

356
00:21:04,700 --> 00:21:09,540
we discussed for the two
previous fungal examples even

357
00:21:09,540 --> 00:21:12,020
overlapping with histoplasma
pretty significantly

358
00:21:12,020 --> 00:21:16,480
in its distribution pattern
in the u.s with most cases

359
00:21:16,480 --> 00:21:20,480
of blastomycosis in endemic
areas that include the

360
00:21:20,480 --> 00:21:24,840
southeastern through
midwestern states and along the

361
00:21:24,840 --> 00:21:28,100
mississippi river up into
canada as you can see on

362
00:21:28,100 --> 00:21:32,820
the map here now like the
other examples blastomyces

363
00:21:32,820 --> 00:21:36,330
is naturally found in
soil and exhibits thermal

364
00:21:36,330 --> 00:21:40,320
dimorphism, with the yeast
phase seen during infections.

365
00:21:40,940 --> 00:21:44,680
It's hyphae-produced canidia
that are said to be slightly

366
00:21:44,680 --> 00:21:47,960
pear-shaped, which is
shown in the upper image

367
00:21:47,960 --> 00:21:51,780
here, and those are going to
give rise to yeast cells that

368
00:21:51,780 --> 00:21:55,600
are typically multi-nucleated
and have thick walls.

369
00:21:55,860 --> 00:21:59,940
Now, as the yeast divide,
they'll display a broad

370
00:21:59,940 --> 00:22:03,320
-based budding process
like you could see here in

371
00:22:03,320 --> 00:22:06,980
the lower microscopic
image versus a narrow-based

372
00:22:06,980 --> 00:22:10,020
bud that you would see
in other fungal species.

373
00:22:13,100 --> 00:22:15,640
Besides asymptomatic cases,

374
00:22:15,900 --> 00:22:19,460
blastomycosis can manifest
as acute or chronic

375
00:22:19,460 --> 00:22:22,640
pneumonia or as an
extra-pulmonary disease.

376
00:22:23,160 --> 00:22:26,840
When canidia are
inhaled, which happens

377
00:22:26,840 --> 00:22:29,080
oftentimes because
of activities

378
00:22:29,080 --> 00:22:31,040
that are going to
disturb the soil,

379
00:22:31,260 --> 00:22:33,880
some of those canidia
will transition

380
00:22:33,880 --> 00:22:35,840
into the budding yeast form.

381
00:22:36,020 --> 00:22:40,500
The larger size, the
thick walls, and surface

382
00:22:40,500 --> 00:22:43,820
glycoproteins of those
yeasts will help them avoid

383
00:22:43,820 --> 00:22:47,420
immediate phagocytosis
by alveolar macrophages.

384
00:22:48,100 --> 00:22:50,800
Now, the primary
defense relies on

385
00:22:50,800 --> 00:22:53,380
neutrophils and cell
-mediated immunity.

386
00:22:53,860 --> 00:22:57,120
So, as was the case
with our other fungal

387
00:22:57,120 --> 00:22:59,840
examples, the cell
-mediated response is again

388
00:22:59,840 --> 00:23:02,100
important for dealing
with the infection.

389
00:23:02,560 --> 00:23:05,120
Now, due to the
size of the yeast,

390
00:23:05,120 --> 00:23:06,880
it makes phagocytosis
difficult.

391
00:23:06,880 --> 00:23:09,620
So, instead, recruited
neutrophils will

392
00:23:09,620 --> 00:23:12,400
degranulate into the
surrounding tissue.

393
00:23:12,620 --> 00:23:15,460
And the resulting dead
cells and cellular

394
00:23:15,460 --> 00:23:18,860
debris leads to the
classic pyogranulomatous

395
00:23:18,860 --> 00:23:21,840
reaction, which are
pus-filled granulomas

396
00:23:21,840 --> 00:23:24,840
in the lungs and in
other affected tissues.

397
00:23:25,400 --> 00:23:28,760
Now, if the immune
response is lacking,

398
00:23:28,900 --> 00:23:31,920
the yeast can disseminate
to extra-pulmonary

399
00:23:31,920 --> 00:23:35,520
sites, and it has a strong
affinity for specific

400
00:23:35,520 --> 00:23:38,580
tissues like the skin,
which you can see here

401
00:23:38,580 --> 00:23:41,880
in the image. And that's
the most frequent site

402
00:23:41,880 --> 00:23:44,860
of disseminated disease.
And that's going to

403
00:23:44,860 --> 00:23:48,480
be followed by the bones,
genitourinary tract,

404
00:23:48,480 --> 00:23:51,400
and even sometimes the
central nervous system.

405
00:23:54,710 --> 00:23:57,060
Diagnosis can be based
on the identification

406
00:23:57,060 --> 00:24:00,140
of thick-walled yeast
cells with single

407
00:24:00,140 --> 00:24:02,740
broad-based buds
seen microscopically.

408
00:24:03,390 --> 00:24:06,040
Sputum microscopy,
like you see here in

409
00:24:06,040 --> 00:24:08,340
the image, is a
simple and inexpensive

410
00:24:08,340 --> 00:24:11,340
test that has high
diagnostic yield.

411
00:24:12,040 --> 00:24:16,480
Culturing is also possible
as well as using PCR.

412
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:20,160
Serological testing here
generally is not used

413
00:24:20,160 --> 00:24:23,340
because of a lack of
specificity and the likelihood

414
00:24:23,340 --> 00:24:26,320
of cross-reactivity with
other endemic fungi.

415
00:24:26,860 --> 00:24:30,520
The treatment options
should sound familiar to

416
00:24:30,520 --> 00:24:34,160
you as well. generally
not necessary, but in

417
00:24:34,160 --> 00:24:37,100
certain instances,
itraconazole is a drug of

418
00:24:37,100 --> 00:24:41,060
choice for most patients
with Amphotericin B being

419
00:24:41,060 --> 00:24:44,120
used to treat progressive
or severe disease.

420
00:24:48,040 --> 00:24:51,080
Pericocidioides is
slightly different from our

421
00:24:51,080 --> 00:24:54,380
previous examples because
its distribution is in

422
00:24:54,380 --> 00:24:57,660
Central and South America
rather than North America.

423
00:24:58,400 --> 00:25:01,420
When it's growing in its
yeast form, It can be

424
00:25:01,420 --> 00:25:04,460
identified microscopically
because it will bud from

425
00:25:04,460 --> 00:25:07,200
a large central mother
cell surrounded by

426
00:25:07,200 --> 00:25:11,100
multiple narrow-based smaller
daughter cells, producing

427
00:25:11,100 --> 00:25:13,640
a distinctive ship wheel
type of appearance,

428
00:25:13,640 --> 00:25:16,420
like you can see here in
this microscopic image.

429
00:25:17,420 --> 00:25:21,300
Exposure to the fungus in
the soil is typically going

430
00:25:21,300 --> 00:25:24,240
to occur through
agricultural work and farming

431
00:25:24,240 --> 00:25:27,900
and is much more prevalent
among males than females.

432
00:25:28,440 --> 00:25:31,860
Now, while the primary
site of infection, again,

433
00:25:31,860 --> 00:25:35,580
is in the lungs, yeast can
spread in certain cases.

434
00:25:36,060 --> 00:25:40,940
Patients exposed to paracoxidioides
will form granulomas

435
00:25:40,940 --> 00:25:44,460
at the site of primary
infection, which may contain

436
00:25:44,460 --> 00:25:48,300
viable latent cells,
leading to a chronic form of

437
00:25:48,300 --> 00:25:51,300
the disease that is more
frequently seen in adults.

438
00:25:51,340 --> 00:25:54,600
The acute form is more
often seen in children.

439
00:25:55,100 --> 00:25:59,180
If dissemination
occurs, a common site is

440
00:25:59,180 --> 00:26:01,500
going to be the oral
mucosa, the lips,

441
00:26:01,500 --> 00:26:04,700
and the nasal cavity,
causing ulcerated

442
00:26:04,700 --> 00:26:06,940
lesions like you can
see here in the images.

443
00:26:07,540 --> 00:26:09,680
Now, in terms of treatment,

444
00:26:09,840 --> 00:26:12,720
itraconazole is
most commonly used.

445
00:26:13,170 --> 00:26:14,880
Sulfonamides can
be used in some

446
00:26:14,880 --> 00:26:17,020
cases, but those
are going to require

447
00:26:17,020 --> 00:26:19,920
prolonged therapy
to prevent relapse.

448
00:26:20,290 --> 00:26:23,300
And Amphotericin B
is typically reserved

449
00:26:23,300 --> 00:26:25,760
for patients with
disseminated disease.

450
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:32,480
Okay, we'll end here
with one final example,

451
00:26:32,820 --> 00:26:36,080
Thaluromyces marnephae,
that fits into the

452
00:26:36,080 --> 00:26:39,000
same category as our
previous examples.

453
00:26:39,040 --> 00:26:43,380
It's found in the soil and
is thermally dimorphic.

454
00:26:43,540 --> 00:26:47,040
However, it's found
in Southeast Asia and

455
00:26:47,040 --> 00:26:49,480
is highly endemic in
places like Thailand

456
00:26:49,480 --> 00:26:52,620
and Vietnam, and
also southern China.

457
00:26:53,340 --> 00:26:56,260
This again affects
immunocompromised

458
00:26:56,260 --> 00:26:58,220
individuals much
more frequently,

459
00:26:58,340 --> 00:27:00,840
especially those
with HIV infection,

460
00:27:00,840 --> 00:27:02,740
which is a major risk factor.

461
00:27:03,580 --> 00:27:06,140
Dissemination has the
potential to affect

462
00:27:06,150 --> 00:27:09,720
many areas in the body,
with skin lesions being

463
00:27:09,720 --> 00:27:12,660
the most common
manifestation, and that's

464
00:27:12,660 --> 00:27:14,920
often accompanied by
weight loss and fever.

465
00:27:16,290 --> 00:27:19,400
Diagnosis can be made by
viewing the elongated yeast

466
00:27:19,400 --> 00:27:23,160
cells microscopically that
have a clearly defined

467
00:27:23,160 --> 00:27:26,480
central septum, like you
can see in the middle of

468
00:27:26,480 --> 00:27:29,860
this image here, where you
have one isolated yeast

469
00:27:29,860 --> 00:27:33,480
cell with its prominent dark
transverse septum scene.

470
00:27:34,480 --> 00:27:36,900
A definitive
diagnosis could also

471
00:27:36,900 --> 00:27:38,680
be made by culturing
it as well.

472
00:27:40,560 --> 00:27:44,760
So ptalaromycosis can have
a relatively high mortality

473
00:27:44,760 --> 00:27:49,020
rate if it goes untreated,
and similar to many of

474
00:27:49,020 --> 00:27:51,980
the other fungal pathogens
we discussed here already,

475
00:27:52,280 --> 00:27:55,780
amphotericin B and
itraconazole are the

476
00:27:55,780 --> 00:27:58,520
agents that are going
to typically be used. So

477
00:28:12,270 --> 00:28:14,590
that concludes the
lecture. I just wanted

478
00:28:14,590 --> 00:28:16,710
to thank everyone
for listening, and I

479
00:28:16,710 --> 00:28:18,890
wanted to provide the
link for feedback.